WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:07.729
I'm going to talk a little bit about the improved
standalone BLAST databases and emphasize the

00:00:07.729 --> 00:00:11.089
ability to get taxonomic information from
them directly.

00:00:11.089 --> 00:00:14.879
We will learn about the new versions of BLAST
and BLAST databases.

00:00:14.879 --> 00:00:19.039
How to limit your search by taxonomy using
information built into the BLAST databases

00:00:19.039 --> 00:00:24.840
. We will learn about searching sequences
by accession faster, using accession lists,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:25.840
or seqid lists.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:29.679
And we will use a program that is part of
the blast package to retrieve sequences by

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:34.240
taxonomy from blast databases, which is a
handy feature.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:41.660
The main motivation for us making the version
5 blast databases is as you probably know

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:47.770
NCBI is preparing for a future where we do
not have G.I. numbers, and blast needs to

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.510
deal with that.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.410
That is part of the motivation for making
these databases.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:57.580
Other features are useful right away for people.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:01.450
These have much faster lookup of accessions
and identifiers and that's part of getting

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.470
ready for the GI-less identifiers.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:07.640
It also includes mapped taxonomy identifiers
for all sequences in the database.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.580
That is the main thing we will take advantage
of today.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.720
These are available as an alpha release on
our FTP site and there is a link to the directory

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:19.400
that contains the executables for 2.8.0+.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:20.400
https://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/blast/executables/blast+/2.8.0alpha/

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.970
Those executables are required if you're going
to work with the version 5 databases.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:30.170
There are examples of those available on the
ftp site if you want to try those out.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:34.300
So just to set the stage for what were going
to talk about, one of the most important things

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:40.450
you can do is limit to the smallest database
that contains the sequences of interest.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:45.670
The most common way people do that and the
most biologically useful way to do that is

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:50.280
to limit to a taxonomic group you're interested
in.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:56.440
On the webpages you can enter taxonomy queries,
organism limits.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:03.350
You can select certain organisms for example
I've got green plants selected and I can remove

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:04.422
a subset of that.

00:02:04.422 --> 00:02:08.869
For example I could get rid of the monocots,
the liliopsids.

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:13.739
That would give me a smaller database and
help me to interpret my results.

00:02:13.739 --> 00:02:18.070
If you want to implement this on standalone,
traditionally this is managed by the GI list.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:19.070
Those are going away.

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.540
There are other problems with creating a GI
list and as we know, those are going away.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.159
there are problems with generating gi lists.

00:02:25.159 --> 00:02:30.719
One of the things we recommend to people,
but in some cases is not doable, and that

00:02:30.719 --> 00:02:36.060
is to download a GI list from Entrez.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:40.579
You could do a search for bacteria and get
all of the bacterial proteins this way in

00:02:40.579 --> 00:02:42.290
principle.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:43.290
That is a lot of proteins.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:50.459
It is so many that even these GI lists are
difficult to download.

00:02:50.459 --> 00:02:57.700
So the built in taxonomy gets around the problem
and helps.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:02.400
If you look at the standalone programs, the
restriction options are available for the

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:11.519
search programs and some are available for
blastcmd as well.

00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:17.749
That seqid list works a lot better than it
did in previous versions of BLAST . It can

00:03:17.749 --> 00:03:22.819
be a list of accessions and you need to convert
them to the version 5 format using blastdb_aliastool

00:03:22.819 --> 00:03:25.719
and I will show you how to do that.

00:03:25.719 --> 00:03:30.959
You can also have a negative list.

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:34.980
And then we have all of these various ones
these that work with taxonomy identifiers.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:41.790
These are the integer IDs from the taxonomy
database.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:48.830
You can specify a list of them on the command
line for the ones that you want leave out,

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:50.530
the negative_taxids.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:56.720
You can also make a file, a taxidlist or a
negative taxid list.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.420
Blastdbcmd also lets you retrieve sequences
from the databases using the taxonomy IDs.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.870
So how do you get the taxonomy IDs?

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:14.779
On the web you can do that and these are much
simpler sets, much fewer identifiers.

00:04:14.779 --> 00:04:17.610
Downloading these is easier than downloading
gi lists.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:23.110
For example, if I want all the green plant
GIs, I would use this query, green plants[subtree].

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:29.180
That gives me everything contained within
that taxon and you can download that as a

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:30.580
taxid list.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:32.819
I can use complicated queries if I would like,
Booleans.

00:04:32.819 --> 00:04:38.639
I could get green plants without the liliopsida,
green plants[subtree] NOT liliopsida[subtree].

00:04:38.639 --> 00:04:43.150
And then I can download this as a file on
my hard drive where I have BLAST installed

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.099
and I can use that to limit.

00:04:45.099 --> 00:04:52.470
If you have BLAST installed, by the way that
is about 200,000 IDs for green plants, if

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:59.710
you have BLAST installed, you will have it
installed on a UNIX or UNIX -like system.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.250
People do run this on Windows.

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:06.560
The following options do not work on Windows
because it uses a shell script.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:14.090
There is an included shell script with the
new BLAST alpha release that invokes the NCBI

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:17.319
EDirect package to get the taxonomy IDs.

00:05:17.319 --> 00:05:21.270
It runs the query I just showed you on the
web.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:26.319
It is a very simple script.

00:05:26.319 --> 00:05:31.069
You give it a name like green plants.

00:05:31.069 --> 00:05:37.439
Or you can give it a tax ID that you can get
by searching to find out what the name is.

00:05:37.439 --> 00:05:44.340
It gives me all of the IDs contained in that
group.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:49.069
Those eventually get you to the point where
the sequences are mapped and you can get them

00:05:49.069 --> 00:05:51.880
for your BLAST searches or to retrieve them
from the database.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:57.860
As I said, this option will only be available
for Unix, Linux or cygwin . Partly because

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:01.230
it is a shell script and partly because i
relies on EDirect.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:04.360
So this requires that you also install the
EDirect package.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.310
The instructions are on that link there.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:10.251
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK179288/
Basically there is a script there and you

00:06:10.251 --> 00:06:11.720
can paste that into your window.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:13.849
It will install EDirect for you.

00:06:13.849 --> 00:06:18.550
It only uses esearch, but you need to install
the whole package.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:24.289
For example, if I put the word soybean in
there as an argument to -n, it will give me

00:06:24.289 --> 00:06:29.590
back an output that tells me what the taxid
is, the rank, and so on and so forth and the

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:31.870
scientific name.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:37.449
If I want to get a list of taxids, I first
need to get the parent so I could say green

00:06:37.449 --> 00:06:38.650
plants.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:47.820
And then I would pass that taxid, to the larger
group, back into get_species_taxid and it

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:52.509
would give me back a list of the taxids that
are in that.

00:06:52.509 --> 00:06:59.229
Of course I could redirect that output to
a file and that would be my taxid list argument,

00:06:59.229 --> 00:07:01.880
the name of that file.

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:07.749
The other thing with the string lookups making
things faster, it does allow for faster searches

00:07:07.749 --> 00:07:09.780
using accession lists.

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:15.610
These are some data that Tom Madden and others
here have compiled looking at comparisons

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:18.610
for how fast things are with the new databases.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:24.990
You will notice with the accession list it
is as fast or faster than searching with the

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:29.550
gi list, which was not the case before.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:33.189
One of the things about this, if you get a
list of accessions you need to convert them

00:07:33.189 --> 00:07:34.919
into a binary format.

00:07:34.919 --> 00:07:40.729
There is utility that comes with the blast
package called blastdb_aliastool and basically

00:07:40.729 --> 00:07:47.909
it will turn this into a version 5 seqid list
which is binary and sorted in some way.

