00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:34.530
That will make it into this seqidlist . That
doesn't take long at all.

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:41.560
And the last thing I'm going to demonstrate
is to run the search limiting to those human

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:43.370
non-model reference sequences.

00:18:43.370 --> 00:18:49.540
Again, it's a blastp command line exactly
like we have used before but the only difference

00:18:49.540 --> 00:19:03.530
is that we have the seqidlist argument which
we did not have before.

00:19:03.530 --> 00:19:09.720
Let's take a look at that.

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:11.880
This is a small set of results.

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:17.350
They match the protein from the fission yeast
and so that is a nice BLAST output.

00:19:17.350 --> 00:19:20.420
I will go back to the PowerPoint slide.

00:19:20.420 --> 00:19:27.470
Basically what we did was talk about BLAST
dbv5 and 2.8.0+.

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:33.290
It works faster with string lookups and it
has much easier options for taxonomic filtering

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:37.450
of the BLAST databases and it works faster
with the accession list.

00:19:37.450 --> 00:19:40.890
There's no need to use the GI list any longer.

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:49.050
This last slide has links to useful material
for EDirect, BLAST and so on.

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:55.420
If you have general questions you might want
to check our NCBI Insights blog or our Learn

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:59.510
page and Fact sheets and our YouTube channel.

00:19:59.510 --> 00:20:09.530
You can write to me, peter.cooper@nih.gov
or our help desk, info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov, or

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:10.530
blast-help.

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:18.450
I will throw it open I think there have been
a lot of questions.

00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:22.940
[Rana Morris] So far there are three pretty
good questions that everyone might like to

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:25.880
hear the answer on.

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:32.300
There will be a Q&amp;A document that we will
get all of those in the know to answer and

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:33.809
post those on the web.

00:20:33.809 --> 00:20:38.860
The first one is, this happens to be an alpha
release someone noticed and wanted to know

00:20:38.860 --> 00:20:41.320
when the final version might be released.

00:20:41.320 --> 00:20:44.470
[Peter Cooper] I don't think we know that.

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:48.200
I think the idea is we will leave it up there
for a while.

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:52.970
How long that it's going to be may depend
on how much feedback we get.

00:20:52.970 --> 00:20:57.220
We are trying to get people to try it out
and tell us what they find.

00:20:57.220 --> 00:21:02.100
I encourage you to do that and let us know
what you think and let us know what we can

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:03.700
do to make it work better for you.

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:09.970
[Rana] The second question, will the BLAST
API be expanded to support these new features?

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:12.429
[Peter] So, let's see.

00:21:12.429 --> 00:21:17.100
I think that is what I was going to say too.

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:19.160
We have no plans to do that right now.

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:23.750
If there is interest in doing that, let us
know and we will add that to the list of things

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:24.750
we need to do.

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:30.280
[Rana] The last question I currently have,
if we are currently downloading the nr database

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:35.880
from the FTP, do we have to format it with
taxonomy information or is it all ready formatted

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:36.880
and ready to go?

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:40.210
[Peter] A couple things.

00:21:40.210 --> 00:21:45.660
The one on the FTP directory that I pointed
you to is a different one than a normal one.

00:21:45.660 --> 00:21:50.090
The one that's up there in the ordinary BLAST
directory where you've always downloaded the

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:54.840
database is the version 4 and the taxonomy
information is not in that database.

00:21:54.840 --> 00:22:01.220
There is no way that I know of for you to
add it to that database.

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:04.880
Right, the version 5 one, the link that I
sent you, those have taxonomy information,

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:07.290
but the version 4 do not.

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:09.560
There's no way to make those into version
5.

00:22:09.560 --> 00:22:12.470
That is it for today.

